1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,736
When a subsea production well starts flowing,

2
00:00:02,836 --> 00:00:06,575
hot oil from the reservoir warms every steel string around it,

3
00:00:06,675 --> 00:00:08,680
and the steel wants to get longer.

4
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:11,219
Normally the wellhead is allowed to move.

5
00:00:11,319 --> 00:00:13,820
It rises a few centimetres off the seabed.

6
00:00:14,383 --> 00:00:15,829
But what if you don't let it?

7
00:00:15,929 --> 00:00:19,879
What if the wellhead and the conductor are held, so they cannot move upward?

8
00:00:19,979 --> 00:00:21,372
Where does the expansion go?

9
00:00:21,472 --> 00:00:22,810
And does the casing buckle?

10
00:00:23,383 --> 00:00:26,505
In this video we build a well with standard casing sizes,

11
00:00:26,605 --> 00:00:31,083
apply the production temperature, and follow the physics all the way to the loads

12
00:00:31,183 --> 00:00:32,043
and the buckling.

13
00:00:33,417 --> 00:00:34,573
Here is our example.

14
00:00:34,673 --> 00:00:38,280
An oil producer in three hundred and fifty metres of water.

15
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:41,295
The thirty inch conductor, with a one inch wall,

16
00:00:41,395 --> 00:00:44,687
is drilled and cemented eighty metres into the seabed.

17
00:00:44,787 --> 00:00:47,514
It carries the low pressure wellhead housing.

18
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:52,563
The twenty inch surface casing, one hundred and thirty three pounds per foot,

19
00:00:52,663 --> 00:00:57,115
runs to seven hundred metres and is cemented all the way back to the seabed.

20
00:00:57,215 --> 00:01:01,668
On top of it sits the eighteen and three quarter inch high pressure wellhead

21
00:01:01,768 --> 00:01:05,262
housing, which lands and locks inside the conductor housing.

22
00:01:05,783 --> 00:01:09,872
Next comes the thirteen and three eighths inch intermediate casing,

23
00:01:09,972 --> 00:01:14,623
seventy two pounds per foot, grade L eighty, set at eighteen hundred metres.

24
00:01:14,723 --> 00:01:17,686
Only the bottom five hundred metres are cemented.

25
00:01:17,786 --> 00:01:22,313
Above the top of cement, thirteen hundred metres of pipe hang free in mud.

26
00:01:22,850 --> 00:01:25,968
Then the nine and five eighths inch production casing,

27
00:01:26,068 --> 00:01:29,305
fifty three and a half pounds per foot, grade P one ten,

28
00:01:29,405 --> 00:01:32,345
set at three thousand two hundred and fifty metres,

29
00:01:32,445 --> 00:01:36,039
with its top of cement at two thousand five hundred and fifty.

30
00:01:36,139 --> 00:01:40,390
A seven inch liner covers the reservoir, and five and a half inch tubing,

31
00:01:40,490 --> 00:01:43,012
with a production packer, brings the oil up.

32
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:46,253
The spaces between the strings are the annuli.

33
00:01:46,353 --> 00:01:50,032
The A annulus is between the tubing and the production casing.

34
00:01:50,132 --> 00:01:52,408
B is outside the nine and five eighths,

35
00:01:52,508 --> 00:01:55,334
and C is outside the thirteen and three eighths.

36
00:01:55,434 --> 00:01:58,685
Each top of cement sits below the previous casing shoe,

37
00:01:58,785 --> 00:02:03,744
so the B and C annuli can bleed into the formation instead of building up pressure.

38
00:02:04,283 --> 00:02:08,982
The key feature for this problem is the free, uncemented length of each string.

39
00:02:09,082 --> 00:02:11,473
Each one is a long, slender steel column,

40
00:02:11,573 --> 00:02:15,604
fixed in cement at the bottom and hung from the wellhead at the top.

41
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:18,720
Let's zoom in on the wellhead.

42
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:23,643
Each casing string hangs from a hanger that lands on a load shoulder inside the

43
00:02:23,743 --> 00:02:25,014
high pressure housing.

44
00:02:25,114 --> 00:02:28,317
A seal assembly and a lockdown ring hold it in place.

45
00:02:28,417 --> 00:02:30,935
So every string is tied to the same point.

46
00:02:31,483 --> 00:02:35,993
Downward loads, like hanging weight, travel through the hangers into the housing,

47
00:02:36,093 --> 00:02:39,351
then into the cemented twenty inch and thirty inch strings,

48
00:02:39,451 --> 00:02:40,830
and finally into the soil.

49
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:46,147
If a hot string pushes upward instead, its hanger pushes up against the lockdown,

50
00:02:46,247 --> 00:02:49,653
and that force must be carried by the very same structure.

51
00:02:50,983 --> 00:02:52,217
Now, the temperature.

52
00:02:52,317 --> 00:02:57,109
Before production, everything sits at the undisturbed geothermal temperature.

53
00:02:57,209 --> 00:03:02,127
Six degrees at the seabed, rising three point three degrees per hundred metres,

54
00:03:02,227 --> 00:03:05,875
to about one hundred and eighteen degrees at the reservoir.

55
00:03:06,417 --> 00:03:10,601
This is also the temperature at which the cement set around each casing.

56
00:03:10,701 --> 00:03:13,041
So it is the stress free reference state.

57
00:03:13,141 --> 00:03:17,801
Every load in this problem comes from the change in temperature from this state.

58
00:03:18,950 --> 00:03:23,611
In production, about three thousand cubic metres of liquid per day flows up the

59
00:03:23,711 --> 00:03:24,033
tubing.

60
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:27,951
It moves so fast that it loses only about ten degrees on the way,

61
00:03:28,051 --> 00:03:31,989
and arrives at the wellhead at about one hundred and eight degrees.

62
00:03:32,089 --> 00:03:36,811
The gap between the flowing temperature and the rock is largest near the seabed,

63
00:03:36,911 --> 00:03:38,378
where the rock is coldest.

64
00:03:38,917 --> 00:03:43,793
That heat flows radially outward, through every layer, to the cold formation.

65
00:03:43,893 --> 00:03:47,736
Each layer is a thermal resistance, like resistors in series.

66
00:03:47,836 --> 00:03:50,321
The formation itself is the largest one,

67
00:03:50,421 --> 00:03:55,039
so the casings end up much closer to the oil temperature than to the rock

68
00:03:55,139 --> 00:03:55,815
temperature.

69
00:03:56,350 --> 00:03:59,304
Just below the seabed, after a year of production,

70
00:03:59,404 --> 00:04:02,419
the tubing has warmed by about one hundred degrees,

71
00:04:02,519 --> 00:04:04,984
the nine and five eighths by ninety seven,

72
00:04:05,084 --> 00:04:09,870
the thirteen and three eighths by eighty seven, the twenty inch by seventy five,

73
00:04:09,970 --> 00:04:11,885
and the conductor by about sixty.

74
00:04:12,417 --> 00:04:16,452
Deeper down the warming fades, and it reaches zero at the reservoir.

75
00:04:16,552 --> 00:04:18,824
The inner strings respond within hours.

76
00:04:18,924 --> 00:04:22,352
The outer strings and the rock warm over weeks and months,

77
00:04:22,452 --> 00:04:25,393
so the loads keep creeping up long after start up.

78
00:04:27,517 --> 00:04:32,395
Steel expands by about twelve point four millionths of its length for every degree.

79
00:04:32,495 --> 00:04:34,555
Integrate that over the free length.

80
00:04:34,655 --> 00:04:39,173
The thirteen and three eighths, warmed by seventy one degrees on average over

81
00:04:39,273 --> 00:04:43,852
thirteen hundred metres, would grow one point one four metres if it were free.

82
00:04:43,952 --> 00:04:47,151
The nine and five eighths would grow almost two metres.

83
00:04:47,683 --> 00:04:49,363
But the string is not free.

84
00:04:49,463 --> 00:04:53,978
The bottom is locked in cement, and the top is locked in the wellhead.

85
00:04:54,078 --> 00:04:59,318
If the length cannot change, the thermal strain must be cancelled by an equal and

86
00:04:59,418 --> 00:05:01,098
opposite mechanical strain.

87
00:05:01,198 --> 00:05:03,405
And mechanical strain means stress.

88
00:05:03,505 --> 00:05:06,240
Sigma equals E, times alpha, times delta T.

89
00:05:06,783 --> 00:05:11,736
For steel, that is about two and a half megapascals of compression for every degree.

90
00:05:11,836 --> 00:05:16,548
Multiply by the steel area, and every degree adds thirty four kilonewtons in the

91
00:05:16,648 --> 00:05:20,878
thirteen and three eighths, and twenty six in the nine and five eighths.

92
00:05:20,978 --> 00:05:25,209
Fully restrained, that is two point four, and one point six meganewtons.

93
00:05:26,550 --> 00:05:30,110
So how much does the wellhead actually move in a normal well?

94
00:05:30,210 --> 00:05:32,091
Think of each string as a spring.

95
00:05:32,191 --> 00:05:34,251
A long free casing is a soft spring.

96
00:05:34,351 --> 00:05:39,171
The thirteen and three eighths has an axial stiffness of about two meganewtons per

97
00:05:39,271 --> 00:05:39,532
metre.

98
00:05:39,632 --> 00:05:43,972
The conductor and the cemented surface casing, gripped by cement and soil,

99
00:05:44,072 --> 00:05:46,252
are more than a hundred times stiffer.

100
00:05:46,783 --> 00:05:51,850
The hot inner strings push up on the wellhead with about four meganewtons in total.

101
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:54,029
The stiff foundation hardly yields.

102
00:05:54,129 --> 00:05:58,574
At the same time, the conductor and the surface casing expand themselves,

103
00:05:58,674 --> 00:06:01,126
sliding in the soft soil near the seabed.

104
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:05,888
Solve for equilibrium, and the wellhead grows about sixty one millimetres.

105
00:06:05,988 --> 00:06:09,464
Forty four of those come from the foundation's own expansion,

106
00:06:09,564 --> 00:06:12,337
and seventeen from the push of the inner strings.

107
00:06:12,883 --> 00:06:14,733
And here is the surprising part.

108
00:06:14,833 --> 00:06:19,425
Sixty one millimetres is tiny compared with the one point one four metres the

109
00:06:19,525 --> 00:06:21,497
thirteen and three eighths wanted.

110
00:06:21,597 --> 00:06:26,554
So even in a normal subsea well, the inner strings are already ninety to ninety six

111
00:06:26,654 --> 00:06:27,712
percent restrained.

112
00:06:27,812 --> 00:06:31,063
They already carry almost the full thermal compression.

113
00:06:32,383 --> 00:06:36,037
Now let's hold the wellhead and the conductor, so the growth is zero.

114
00:06:36,583 --> 00:06:39,770
The inner strings pick up only the last few percent.

115
00:06:39,870 --> 00:06:43,247
The thirteen and three eighths goes from two point two,

116
00:06:43,347 --> 00:06:46,533
to two point three meganewtons of added compression.

117
00:06:46,633 --> 00:06:51,211
The nine and five eighths, from one point five one, to one point five six.

118
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:53,774
The big change is in the foundation.

119
00:06:53,874 --> 00:06:58,021
The conductor and the surface casing can no longer expand at the seabed,

120
00:06:58,121 --> 00:07:01,030
so their own thermal strain turns into compression.

121
00:07:01,130 --> 00:07:04,687
About one hundred and forty four megapascals in the conductor,

122
00:07:04,787 --> 00:07:07,401
and one hundred and eighty in the twenty inch.

123
00:07:07,501 --> 00:07:11,766
That is almost half of its yield strength, before any other load is added.

124
00:07:12,283 --> 00:07:17,154
And the restraint itself must push down with about nineteen and a half meganewtons.

125
00:07:17,254 --> 00:07:20,149
That is the weight of roughly two thousand tonnes.

126
00:07:20,249 --> 00:07:22,425
Even a partial restraint is expensive.

127
00:07:22,525 --> 00:07:26,857
Cutting the growth in half still takes about eight and a half meganewtons.

128
00:07:27,383 --> 00:07:30,392
Restricting growth does not remove the expansion.

129
00:07:30,492 --> 00:07:32,675
It converts displacement into force.

130
00:07:34,017 --> 00:07:35,631
So, does the casing buckle?

131
00:07:35,731 --> 00:07:38,425
To answer that, we need the effective force.

132
00:07:38,525 --> 00:07:41,791
For a pipe filled with fluid and surrounded by fluid,

133
00:07:41,891 --> 00:07:45,728
buckling is not governed by the true axial force in the steel.

134
00:07:45,828 --> 00:07:48,141
It is governed by the effective force.

135
00:07:48,241 --> 00:07:52,395
The axial force, minus the internal pressure times the inside area,

136
00:07:52,495 --> 00:07:55,570
plus the external pressure times the outside area.

137
00:07:55,670 --> 00:07:57,221
This is Lubinski's result.

138
00:07:58,550 --> 00:08:02,541
Before production, the thirteen and three eighths hangs in tension.

139
00:08:02,641 --> 00:08:07,548
The effective force is largest at the wellhead, about one point three meganewtons,

140
00:08:07,648 --> 00:08:10,357
and falls to almost zero at the top of cement,

141
00:08:10,457 --> 00:08:14,265
because each point only carries the weight of the pipe below it.

142
00:08:14,783 --> 00:08:18,657
Heating adds a uniform compression along the whole free length,

143
00:08:18,757 --> 00:08:20,486
so the entire profile shifts.

144
00:08:20,586 --> 00:08:24,649
For the restrained well, it shifts by two point three meganewtons.

145
00:08:24,749 --> 00:08:29,885
Now the effective force is compressive all the way from the top of cement up to the

146
00:08:29,985 --> 00:08:30,452
wellhead.

147
00:08:30,983 --> 00:08:34,111
In a vertical well, the critical buckling load is tiny.

148
00:08:34,211 --> 00:08:38,924
Under two hundred kilonewtons for this pipe, because the pipe is long and slender,

149
00:08:39,024 --> 00:08:42,211
and there is no low side for gravity to hold it against.

150
00:08:42,311 --> 00:08:44,206
So the compressed section buckles.

151
00:08:44,306 --> 00:08:46,026
But it cannot fly out sideways.

152
00:08:46,126 --> 00:08:47,728
The outer casing confines it.

153
00:08:47,828 --> 00:08:50,897
It first snakes sinusoidally, then wraps into a helix,

154
00:08:50,997 --> 00:08:52,716
pressed against the outer wall.

155
00:08:53,250 --> 00:08:56,014
The nine and five eighths behaves the same way.

156
00:08:56,114 --> 00:09:00,463
But it carries more hanging weight, so only its lower part is compressed,

157
00:09:00,563 --> 00:09:03,875
from about six hundred metres down to the top of cement.

158
00:09:03,975 --> 00:09:08,689
Restraining the wellhead moves its neutral point about a hundred metres higher.

159
00:09:10,017 --> 00:09:14,091
Here is the thirteen and three eighths inside the twenty inch casing,

160
00:09:14,191 --> 00:09:16,269
with the sideways scale exaggerated.

161
00:09:16,369 --> 00:09:19,172
The radial clearance is sixty eight millimetres.

162
00:09:19,750 --> 00:09:22,750
As the compression grows, the helix tightens.

163
00:09:22,850 --> 00:09:27,296
Lubinski's pitch is pi, times the square root of eight E I over F.

164
00:09:27,396 --> 00:09:30,396
About thirty six metres at the top of cement.

165
00:09:30,950 --> 00:09:34,107
Bending the pipe into a helix adds bending stress.

166
00:09:34,207 --> 00:09:37,559
D, times the clearance, times the force, over four I.

167
00:09:37,659 --> 00:09:40,751
Here that is up to about fifty seven megapascals.

168
00:09:40,851 --> 00:09:43,487
The pipe also presses on the outer casing,

169
00:09:43,587 --> 00:09:47,135
with a contact force of about two kilonewtons per metre.

170
00:09:47,235 --> 00:09:51,499
That wears both strings whenever something rotates or moves inside.

171
00:09:52,050 --> 00:09:54,087
Buckling gives back a little length,

172
00:09:54,187 --> 00:09:56,937
because a helix is shorter than a straight pipe.

173
00:09:57,037 --> 00:10:00,795
But only about five centimetres of the one point one four metres.

174
00:10:00,895 --> 00:10:03,051
So buckling does not relieve the load.

175
00:10:04,417 --> 00:10:05,445
Now add it all up.

176
00:10:05,545 --> 00:10:10,584
In the thirteen and three eighths, axial compression, plus bending, plus pressure,

177
00:10:10,684 --> 00:10:14,908
gives a von Mises stress of about two hundred and thirty megapascals.

178
00:10:15,008 --> 00:10:17,916
That is forty two percent of its yield strength.

179
00:10:18,016 --> 00:10:21,363
The nine and five eighths reaches about twenty percent.

180
00:10:21,883 --> 00:10:22,226
So yes.

181
00:10:22,326 --> 00:10:25,197
The casings buckle, helically, and elastically.

182
00:10:25,297 --> 00:10:28,105
And they would do so with a free wellhead too.

183
00:10:28,205 --> 00:10:30,823
The restraint makes it only slightly worse.

184
00:10:31,350 --> 00:10:36,222
What still needs checking are the connections, which must be rated for compression.

185
00:10:36,322 --> 00:10:41,193
The casing hanger lockdown, which now sees about one meganewton pushing up from the

186
00:10:41,293 --> 00:10:42,810
thirteen and three eighths.

187
00:10:42,910 --> 00:10:44,547
Wear from the contact forces.

188
00:10:44,647 --> 00:10:49,100
And above all, the outer strings and the restraint, which take the real hit.

189
00:10:50,417 --> 00:10:55,135
There is one situation where restraint changes everything for a casing string.

190
00:10:55,235 --> 00:10:58,286
When the twenty inch is not cemented to the seabed.

191
00:10:58,386 --> 00:11:01,313
Say its top of cement is two hundred metres down.

192
00:11:01,413 --> 00:11:05,390
Now the twenty inch has its own free length, inside the conductor.

193
00:11:05,917 --> 00:11:08,529
With a free wellhead, the conductor gives way,

194
00:11:08,629 --> 00:11:12,714
and the twenty inch picks up two and a half meganewtons of compression.

195
00:11:12,814 --> 00:11:15,839
Restrained, that jumps to four point two meganewtons.

196
00:11:16,383 --> 00:11:20,087
It buckles into a fifty three metre helix inside the conductor,

197
00:11:20,187 --> 00:11:23,046
with about seventy megapascals of bending stress,

198
00:11:23,146 --> 00:11:26,185
and a combined stress of sixty two percent of yield.

199
00:11:27,517 --> 00:11:28,028
To sum up.

200
00:11:28,128 --> 00:11:30,964
Production heat makes every casing want to grow.

201
00:11:31,064 --> 00:11:35,613
The free lengths are fixed at both ends, so that growth becomes compression,

202
00:11:35,713 --> 00:11:38,304
about two and a half megapascals per degree.

203
00:11:38,850 --> 00:11:42,948
In a subsea well, the inner strings are already almost fully restrained.

204
00:11:43,048 --> 00:11:47,788
They buckle helically in their free sections, whether or not the wellhead can move.

205
00:11:47,888 --> 00:11:51,111
Restraining the wellhead adds only a few percent to them.

206
00:11:51,650 --> 00:11:55,842
The real cost of restraint lands on the conductor, the surface casing,

207
00:11:55,942 --> 00:11:58,724
the wellhead housing, and the restraint itself.

208
00:11:58,824 --> 00:11:59,950
Tens of meganewtons.

209
00:12:00,050 --> 00:12:03,936
If growth must be limited, reduce the heat with insulated tubing,

210
00:12:04,036 --> 00:12:08,964
make sure the surface casing is cemented to the seabed, and design the foundation,

211
00:12:09,064 --> 00:12:13,256
the hanger lockdowns and the connections for the full restrained load.

212
00:12:13,783 --> 00:12:15,798
Thermal expansion is not optional.

213
00:12:15,898 --> 00:12:18,347
Stop the movement, and you get the force.
